<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suez Canal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Suez Canal is a sea-level waterway across the Isthmus of Suez in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. Built by a French-led company under a concession granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-pasha">Said Pasha</a> in 1854, it opened in 1869 and transformed the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and Asia, raising the value of ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> nationalised it in 1956, and it was closed from 1967 to 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suez-canal.jpg" alt="Suez Canal" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iss016e019375.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A railway network, begun in 1853 and among the largest in the world by 1900, irrigation canals in the Punjab and the opening of the <b>Suez Canal</b> in 1869 encouraged exports of cotton, jute, wheat, tea, indigo and opium, while Indian markets absorbed British textiles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Egypt has the oldest continuous parliamentary tradition in the Arab world: its first popular assembly met in 1866, and the <b>Suez Canal</b>, opened in 1869, still earned the state a record $9.4 billion in 2023.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Its economy suffered an immediate shock: the closure of the <b>Suez Canal</b> after the Arab–Israeli war of June 1967 and the departure of the British base removed much of the shipping and spending on which Aden had lived, and the canal did not reopen until 1975.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Steamships, the telegraph and the <b>Suez Canal</b>, opened in 1869, drew the region into the world economy, and European influence, commercial and political, grew with them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…or &quot;Salam Affandina&quot;, dated from the reign of Khedive Ismail: it was played at the opening of the <b>Suez Canal</b> and adopted in 1871, an instrumental piece without official words whose most likely author was Giuseppe Pugioli, an Italian cornetist who had come to Egypt to perform Verdi's…</blockquote><a href="/songs/biladi-biladi-biladi">Biladi, Biladi, Biladi</a></li><li><blockquote>…tune of the best-known chant of the fans of Al Ahly in Cairo against their rivals Zamalek, and of Zamalek's fans against Ahly: it opens with &quot;Hela hela&quot;, a call of encouragement first used by labourers digging the <b>Suez Canal</b>, and goes on to insult the rival club.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ya-mustafa">Ya Mustafa</a></li></ul></section>
